@@s001 Bellman-Ford: shortest paths with negative weights
@@s002 ora:distance updated in this round,dato:distance,no:negative weight
@@s003 Bellman-Ford: shortest paths also with <b>negative</b> weights (there are no negative cycles). Distances: <code>d(s) = 0</code>, others ∞.
@@s004 Round 1: I relax all the edges. s→a: d(a) = 4. s→b: d(b) = 5. a→b with weight −3: 4 − 3 = 1 &lt; 5, so d(b) = 1. A negative weight can improve a distance that is already fixed.
@@s005 Round 2 and the following: the edges reviewed again no longer improve anything. With n nodes <b>n − 1 rounds</b> are enough: every simple path has at most n − 1 edges.
@@s006 The cost is <code>O(n·m)</code> (n − 1 rounds over m edges). A reachable negative cycle is detected with one more round: if a distance still improves, the shortest path does not exist.
@@s007 Bellman-Ford: shortest paths with negative weights
@@s008 Bellman-Ford finds the minimum cost from a source even with edges of negative weight. It is the same DP for paths in a DAG, with a counter of edges.
@@s009 The idea: relax all the edges, n − 1 times
@@s010 A simple shortest path has at most <code>n − 1</code> edges. So it is enough to repeat <code>n − 1</code> times: relax <i>all</i> the edges. Each round fixes paths one edge longer. Equivalent DP: <code>OPT(i, v)</code> = minimum cost from <code>s</code> to <code>v</code> with at most <code>i</code> edges.
@@s011 A negative edge improves an already fixed distance
@@s012 Negative cycles
@@s013 If a reachable cycle has negative total cost, the shortest path does not exist: every round lowers it again. Bellman-Ford detects it with one more round: if a distance still improves, there is a negative cycle.
@@s014 The cost
@@s015 <code>O(n·m)</code> time, <code>O(n)</code> space. Slower than Dijkstra, but it works with negative weights, which Dijkstra does not handle.
@@s016 The code, side by side
@@s017 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
